 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "In 1960, the Royal Air Force parachuted cats into Borneo.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 3.672
  },
  {
   "text": "It started with mosquitoes. Malaria was spreading through the villages.",
   "s": 3.972,
   "e": 7.104
  },
  {
   "text": "So health workers sprayed the houses with the pesticide DDT.",
   "s": 7.404,
   "e": 10.605
  },
  {
   "text": "The mosquitoes died. But so did the wasps that ate caterpillars.",
   "s": 10.905,
   "e": 13.893
  },
  {
   "text": "So caterpillars munched the thatched roofs until they caved in.",
   "s": 14.193,
   "e": 16.818
  },
  {
   "text": "Then it got worse. Lizards ate the poisoned bugs, and cats ate the lizards.",
   "s": 17.118,
   "e": 20.364
  },
  {
   "text": "The cats died, and the rats moved in.",
   "s": 20.664,
   "e": 22.387
  },
  {
   "text": "So the RAF packed live cats into crates and dropped them by parachute.",
   "s": 22.687,
   "e": 26.192
  },
  {
   "text": "The mission's official name? Operation Cat Drop.",
   "s": 26.492,
   "e": 28.77
  }
 ],
